        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "A Harvard Business School, MIT, and University of Washington study found that AI-generated explanations paradoxically worsen human decision quality by suppressing independent judgment. Evaluators given reasoned AI rejections agreed with them more often — even when wrong — due to cognitive biases like negativity bias and the illusion of explanatory depth. Researchers recommend treating AI explanations as behavioral interventions, not transparency tools.",
        "execSummary":  "Enterprises deploying AI decisioning tools must reckon with a counterintuitive finding: giving employees AI rationales makes them less accurate, not more. When AI explained its recommendations, evaluators over-deferred and produced more false negatives, rejecting viable ideas they would have approved on their own. The root cause is cognitive — narrative fluency creates an illusion of understanding that discourages independent verification. Senior leaders should redesign human-AI workflows to actively invite disagreement with AI outputs, not just present them.",
